---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Brief = `How often do we actually need to measure? Every reading costs
storage and I do not want to pay for readings that tell me
nothing new. Show me the trade.`;

const SampleOutput = `  every 20: 3 samples, total error 1013.9
  every 10: 5 samples, total error 915.9
  every 5: 9 samples, total error 419.5
  every 2: 21 samples, total error 102.4
  every 1: 41 samples, total error 0.0`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/cc-set-7" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back to Set 7
        </a>

        <h1>Code Challenge: Sample Rate</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="brief">
          <h2>The Brief</h2>

          <Code code={Brief} frame="code" lang="txt" title="What they asked for" showLineNumbers={false} class="mt-3" />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>Write your requirements list before you write any code.</b> Input,
            calculate, output, and anything you had to decide for yourself. See{" "}
            <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/cc-set-7">Set 7</a> for what that looks like.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="notes">
          <h2>Notes</h2>

          <ul>
            <li><code>signal(t)</code> from Activity 2.16 is your stand-in for the real world. Do not change it &mdash; it represents something you do not control.</li>
            <li>For each sample rate: count the samples, then <b>measure how wrong the reconstruction is</b>. Rebuild a value for every whole <code>t</code> from your samples and add up the gaps.</li>
            <li>How you rebuild the missing values is your decision. Holding the last sample is the simplest; guessing between two is better. Say which you chose.</li>
            <li><b>The output has to show the trade, not just two columns.</b> More samples, less error &mdash; the client wants to know where to stop.</li>
            <li>In your requirements list: name the rate you would recommend and what you gave up to pick it.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="sample-output">
          <h2>Sample Output</h2>

          <Code code={SampleOutput} frame="terminal" title="Sample Output" lang="txt" showLineNumbers={false} class="mt-3" />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Look at the step from 20 to 10.</b> Nearly double the samples and the
            error barely moves. Then 10 to 5 halves it. <b>The saving is not evenly
            spread</b>, and finding where it pays off is the actual challenge.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Your requirements list and your program file, named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">cc-sample-rate</b>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1 class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash">
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
